---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="Building This Site | Jonathan Wideman"
	description="I wanted room for my full creative range, so I built this site from scratch with Claude Code."
>
	<main>
		<p class="back"><a href="/#projects">← Back to projects</a></p>

		<h1>Building This Site</h1>

		<p>
			Website builders make it easy to look good and hard to look like yourself. Every template is
			someone else's idea of a portfolio. I wanted room for my full creative range, so I built this
			site from scratch with Claude Code.
		</p>

		<p>
			I started with basic HTML and CSS. In one week I installed the tools developers use (Git,
			Node, Homebrew and the GitHub CLI), scaffolded the site with Astro, pushed it to GitHub and
			deployed it on Vercel. The first deploy took nine seconds.
		</p>

		<p>
			The hardest part wasn't the code. It was keeping this from becoming an AI website. AI is built
			for speed, so I went slow on purpose. Claude Code handles the build, but the creative calls
			stay with me. Instead of taking the defaults, I researched the visual direction myself. I chose
			Space Grotesk for headlines and Space Mono for body text, for a raw, technical precision that
			breaks standard UI conventions. The palette is international orange, hyper mint, chalk white
			and asphalt black: an off-white base with heavy black borders, and the mint and orange cutting
			through. It reads like an industrial manifest or a raw gallery wall.
		</p>
	</main>
</Layout>

<style>
	main {
		max-width: 42rem;
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: 1.5rem 0 3rem;
	}

	.back {
		margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
		font-size: 0.9375rem;
	}

	h1 {
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
		font-size: 2.25rem;
		line-height: 1.2;
	}
</style>
